\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%% ============================================================

Estimation of distribution algorithms (EDAs)
\citep{Larranhaga_and_Lozano:2002,Lozano_et_al:2005,Muhlenbein_and_Paas:1996,Pelikan_et_al:2006}
replace the variation operators of genetic algorithms by the explicit estimation
and sampling of a probabilistic model.  At each generation a model is learned
from a set of selected solutions, and new solutions are obtained by sampling that
model.  The general description of the EDA paradigm, of the probabilistic
graphical models used in the discrete and continuous cases, and of the modular
architecture on which this library is built, is given in the \texttt{pateda}
user guide and is not repeated here.

Many important combinatorial optimization problems, however, are naturally
defined over the \emph{symmetric group} $\mathcal{S}_n$: the travelling salesman
problem (TSP), the permutation flowshop scheduling problem (PFSP), the linear
ordering problem (LOP), and the quadratic assignment problem (QAP), among many
others.  A solution to such a problem is a permutation $\sigma$ of $n$ items,
i.e.\ a bijection $\sigma: \{1,\ldots,n\} \rightarrow \{1,\ldots,n\}$.  The
number of solutions is $n!$, and, crucially, the set of permutations is
\emph{not} a Cartesian product of independent variable domains.  A model that
treats the $n$ positions of a permutation as $n$ independent integer variables
--- the natural choice for a discrete EDA --- assigns positive probability to
vectors that are not permutations at all, so that sampling produces infeasible
individuals that must be repaired, distorting the distribution the model was
supposed to represent \citep{Ceberio_et_al:2012}.

\texttt{perm\_pateda} therefore replaces the model family, not the algorithm.
The EDA loop, the selection and replacement operators, the statistics and the
stopping criteria are those of \texttt{pateda}; what changes is (i) how a
population of permutations is represented, (ii) which probability distributions
over $\mathcal{S}_n$ are estimated from the selected set, and (iii) how new
permutations are drawn from them --- always guaranteeing feasibility by
construction.  Three complementary strategies are implemented:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Distributions defined directly on $\mathcal{S}_n$.}
    Distance-based exponential models (Mallows, generalized Mallows, kernels of
    Mallows models), multistage models (Plackett--Luce and its mixtures),
    first- and second-order statistics of the population (node and edge histogram
    models), and doubly stochastic matrix models.  Sampling from these models
    always returns a valid permutation.
  \item \textbf{Bijective codings of $\mathcal{S}_n$.}  Lehmer codes,
    Fisher--Yates draw sequences and insertion vectors map $\mathcal{S}_n$
    one-to-one onto a product of bounded integer domains
    $\{0\} \times \{0,1\} \times \cdots \times \{0,\ldots,n-1\}$.  In that space
    \emph{every} vector decodes to a valid permutation, so the whole battery of
    discrete EDA models of \texttt{pateda} (univariate, tree-structured,
    Markov chains) becomes directly applicable.
  \item \textbf{Continuous relaxations.}  Random keys \citep{Bean:1994} encode a
    permutation as a real vector in $[0,1]^n$ whose sorting order defines the
    permutation.  Any continuous EDA of \texttt{pateda} (Gaussian univariate,
    full multivariate Gaussian, vine copulas) can then be used to search the
    permutation space.
\end{itemize}

The main design goals of \texttt{perm\_pateda} follow those of \texttt{pateda}:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Plug-and-play convenience:} classes such as
    \texttt{MallowsKendallEDA}, \texttt{EHMEDA}, \texttt{PlackettLuceEDA} or
    \texttt{DSMASEDA} provide a one-liner interface to a complete permutation
    EDA.
  \item \emph{Flexible component assembly:} every learning and sampling method is
    an independent object with the \texttt{pateda} component interface, so it can
    be plugged into a generic \texttt{EDA} through \texttt{EDAComponents}.
  \item \emph{Extensibility:} adding a new permutation model requires only a
    learner (selected population $\rightarrow$ model dictionary) and a sampler
    (model dictionary $\rightarrow$ new permutations).
  \item \emph{Single- and multi-objective support:} a decomposition-based
    multi-objective framework (MEDA/D) is provided and can be instantiated with
    any of nine permutation models.
  \item \emph{Reproducibility:} instance readers for the standard benchmark
    libraries and statistical comparison utilities (Friedman, pairwise Wilcoxon,
    critical-difference diagrams) are included.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this guide is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:install} describes installation and the package structure.
Section~\ref{sec:eda} explains how a permutation EDA is defined and executed.
Section~\ref{sec:algorithms} surveys the plug-and-play algorithm classes.
Section~\ref{sec:models} details the probability models over permutations, their
