\section{Defining and Executing a Permutation EDA}
\label{sec:eda}
%% ============================================================

\subsection{General Description}

A permutation EDA follows the generic EDA schema of \texttt{pateda}
(Algorithm~\ref{alg:PermEDA}): an initial population of permutations is generated
and evaluated; at each generation a subset of the population is selected, a
probability model over $\mathcal{S}_n$ is estimated from it, and new permutations
are sampled from the model to form the next population.  The only structural
differences with respect to the discrete EDA of \texttt{pateda} are that the
initialization returns permutations, and that the learning/sampling pair operates
on $\mathcal{S}_n$.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Estimation of distribution algorithm for permutation problems}
\label{alg:PermEDA}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $t \leftarrow 0$
\State Generate $D_0$: $N$ permutations drawn uniformly at random from
  $\mathcal{S}_n$
\State Evaluate $D_0$
\Repeat
  \State $D_t^{Se} \leftarrow$ Select $M \leq N$ permutations from $D_t$
  \State $p_t(\sigma) \leftarrow$ Estimate a probability distribution over
    $\mathcal{S}_n$ from $D_t^{Se}$
  \State $D_{t+1} \leftarrow$ Sample $N$ permutations from $p_t(\sigma)$ and
    evaluate them
  \State $t \leftarrow t+1$
\Until{stopping criterion is met}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Two execution paths are available.  The \emph{component path} builds a
\texttt{pateda.core.eda.EDA} object out of an \texttt{EDAComponents} dataclass in
which the learning and sampling slots are filled with \texttt{perm\_pateda}
objects; this gives access to the whole \texttt{pateda} machinery (arbitrary
selection and replacement operators, stopping conditions, statistics tracking,
local optimization, caching).  The \emph{wrapper path} uses the plug-and-play
classes of Section~\ref{sec:algorithms}, which implement a self-contained loop.

\subsection{The Permutation EDA Wrappers}

All plug-and-play classes derive from an internal base class \texttt{\_PermEDA}
(\texttt{algorithms/permutation.py}) that implements the loop of
Algorithm~\ref{alg:PermEDA} with truncation selection and optional elitism.  A
self-contained loop is used --- instead of delegating to
\texttt{pateda.core.eda.EDA} --- because several permutation learners and
samplers expose only \texttt{\_\_call\_\_} (rather than \texttt{.learn()} /
\texttt{.sample()}), and because the samplers need an explicit
\texttt{sample\_size} argument.  The base class bridges both calling conventions
transparently.

\subsubsection{Input Parameters}

All permutation algorithm classes share the following constructor parameters:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{9cm}}
\toprule
Parameter & Description \\
\midrule
\texttt{n\_vars} & Permutation length $n$; items are $0,\ldots,n-1$ \\
\texttt{fitness\_func} & Callable mapping a 1-D integer array (a permutation) to
  a scalar; higher is better \\
\texttt{pop\_size} & Population size $N$ (default: 100) \\
\texttt{n\_gen} & Number of generations (default: 50) \\
\texttt{selection\_ratio} & Truncation fraction $M/N$ (default: 0.5) \\
\texttt{elitism} & Preserve the best permutation found so far (default: True) \\
\texttt{random\_seed} & Seed of the \texttt{numpy} random generator
  (default: None) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent Model-specific parameters are added by individual classes and are
listed in Table~\ref{tab:perm_algs}.

\subsubsection{Output: \texttt{Statistics} and \texttt{Cache}}

\texttt{run()} returns the pair \texttt{(stats, cache)} of \texttt{pateda}
objects.  \texttt{Statistics} accumulates, per generation, the best, mean and
worst fitness, the population diversity measures, and the best individual found
overall (\texttt{stats.best\_fitness\_overall}, \texttt{stats.best\_individual}).
Their fields and the \texttt{Cache} contents are documented in the
\texttt{pateda} user guide; the permutation wrappers populate them with exactly
the same semantics.

\subsection{Assembling a Permutation EDA from Components}

Because the learners and samplers implement the \texttt{pateda}
\texttt{LearningMethod} / \texttt{SamplingMethod} interfaces (directly, or through
the adapters in \texttt{algorithms/base.py}), a permutation EDA can be assembled
component by component exactly like any other \texttt{pateda} algorithm:

\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np
from pateda.core.eda import EDA
from pateda.core.components import EDAComponents
from pateda.selection import TruncationSelection
from pateda.replacement import ElitistReplacement
from pateda.stop_conditions import MaxGenerations

from perm_pateda.seeding import PermutationInit
from perm_pateda.learning.histogram import LearnEHM
from perm_pateda.sampling.histogram import SampleEHM
from perm_pateda.functions import create_random_tsp

n_cities, pop_size, n_gen = 15, 80, 40
tsp = create_random_tsp(n_cities, seed=42)

components = EDAComponents(
    seeding=PermutationInit(),
    selection=TruncationSelection(ratio=0.5),
    learning=LearnEHM(),
    sampling=SampleEHM(),
    replacement=ElitistReplacement(n_elite=int(pop_size * 0.1)),
    stop_condition=MaxGenerations(n_gen),
)
components.learning_params = {"symmetric": True, "beta_ratio": 0.01}
components.sampling_params = {"sample_size": pop_size}

eda = EDA(n_vars=n_cities,
          cardinality=np.full(n_cities, n_cities, dtype=int),
          fitness_func=tsp, pop_size=pop_size,
          components=components, random_seed=42)
stats, cache = eda.run()
print("Best tour length:", -stats.best_fitness_overall)
\end{lstlisting}

Two remarks.  The \texttt{learning\_params} and \texttt{sampling\_params}
dictionaries carry the model-specific arguments that the generic executor does
not know about --- in particular \texttt{sample\_size}, which the permutation
samplers require.  And the \texttt{cardinality} argument, which is essential for
discrete EDAs, carries no information here: it is passed for interface
compatibility only, and the plug-and-play wrappers fill it with the placeholder
\texttt{np.arange(n\_vars)}.

\subsection{Example: Mallows EDA on the Linear Ordering Problem}

The equivalent program using a plug-and-play wrapper is a single object
construction:

\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np
from perm_pateda import MallowsKendallEDA
from perm_pateda.functions import create_random_lop

lop = create_random_lop(15, seed=0)

alg = MallowsKendallEDA(
    n_vars=15,
    fitness_func=lop,          # LOP.__call__: permutation -> scalar
    pop_size=100,
    n_gen=50,
    selection_ratio=0.3,
    random_seed=0,
)
stats, _ = alg.run(verbose=True)
print("Best fitness:    ", stats.best_fitness_overall)
print("Best permutation:", stats.best_individual)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Fitness Convention}

As in \texttt{pateda}, \textbf{the fitness function is always maximized}.  The
problem classes of Section~\ref{sec:functions} follow this convention
internally: \texttt{TSP.\_\_call\_\_} returns the \emph{negated} tour length,
\texttt{QAP.\_\_call\_\_} the negated assignment cost, \texttt{PFSP.\_\_call\_\_}
the negated makespan or flowtime, and \texttt{MVC.\_\_call\_\_} the negated size
of the vertex cover, while \texttt{LOP}, \texttt{MIS} and \texttt{MaxCut} return
directly maximized quantities.  Each class additionally provides an
\texttt{evaluate\_*} method returning the natural (positive, non-negated)
objective value for reporting:

\begin{lstlisting}
tour_length  = tsp.evaluate_distance(perm)   # positive
qap_cost     = qap.evaluate_cost(perm)       # positive
makespan     = pfsp.evaluate_makespan(perm)  # positive
lop_value    = lop.evaluate_objective(perm)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Indexing Convention}

Permutations are internally 0-indexed arrays containing each of
$0,\ldots,n-1$ exactly once.  For compatibility with instance files and with the
Matlab toolbox, the learners, samplers and problem classes detect 1-indexed
input (by testing whether the minimum entry equals one) and convert it
internally.  Users writing new components should produce 0-indexed
permutations.

