\section{Probability Models over Permutations}
\label{sec:models}
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Permutation Representation and Notation}

Permutations are denoted by $\sigma$ or $\pi$; $\pi^{-1}$ is the inverse of
$\pi$; the composition of $\sigma$ and $\pi$ is $\sigma\pi$, defined by
$\sigma\pi(i) = \sigma(\pi(i))$; $e = 1\,2\,3\cdots n$ is the identity
permutation.  A distance $d(\sigma,\pi)$ measures the disagreement between the
two permutations and, for convenience, $d(\sigma,e)$ is written $d(\sigma)$.  All
the distances considered here are \emph{right invariant},
\begin{equation}
d(\sigma, \pi) = d(\sigma\pi^{-1}, e) = d(\sigma\pi^{-1}) ,
\end{equation}
a property that is repeatedly exploited by the learning and sampling algorithms.

A population of $N$ permutations of length $n$ is stored as a 2-D
\texttt{numpy} array of shape $(N, n)$ and integer \texttt{dtype}, in the same
way that \texttt{pateda} stores a discrete population.

\subsection{Distances on Permutations}

The module \texttt{distances.py} implements four distances together with the
auxiliary decompositions required by the models.

\paragraph{Kendall's-$\tau$ distance.}
$d_\tau(\sigma,\pi)$ counts the number of pairwise disagreements between
$\sigma$ and $\pi$:
\begin{align*}
d_\tau(\sigma, \pi)=|\{(i,j) : i<j,\,\, & (\sigma(i) < \sigma(j) \land \pi(i)>\pi(j) ) \\
 \lor & (\pi(i) < \pi(j) \land \sigma(i)>\sigma(j) )  \, \}| .
\end{align*}
It is the best choice when permutations are interpreted as \emph{rankings} (as in
recommender systems), and it is equivalent to the minimum number of adjacent
swaps needed to convert $\sigma^{-1}$ into $\pi^{-1}$.  The Kendall's-$\tau$
distance decomposes into the \emph{inversion vector}
$\mathbf{V}(\sigma) = (V_1(\sigma),\ldots,V_{n-1}(\sigma))$,
\begin{equation}
 V_j(\sigma) = \sum_{i=j+1}^n \bm{1}_{[\sigma(i) < \sigma(j)]} ,
 \label{eq:kendall_decomp}
\end{equation}
so that $V_j(\sigma)$ is the number of items with index larger than $j$ whose
value is smaller than $\sigma(j)$, with $0 \leq V_j(\sigma) \leq n-j$.  For
example, if $\sigma = 2\,1\,3\,6\,4\,5$, then $\mathbf{V}(\sigma) = (1\,0\,0\,2\,0)$
and $d_\tau(\sigma) = \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} V_j(\sigma) = 3$.  The conversion between
$\mathbf{V}(\sigma)$ and $\sigma$ takes $O(n^2)$.  In \texttt{perm\_pateda} the
inversion vector is computed by \texttt{\_v\_vector} and the distance by
\texttt{kendall\_distance}.

\paragraph{Cayley distance.}
$d_c(\sigma,\pi)$ counts the minimum number of swaps (not necessarily adjacent)
needed to transform $\sigma$ into $\pi$.  It is intimately related to the cycles
of $\sigma$ --- ordered sets $\{i_1,\ldots,i_s\}$ such that $\sigma(i_1)=i_2$,
$\sigma(i_2)=i_3$, \ldots, $\sigma(i_s)=i_1$ --- and when the reference
permutation is the identity, $d_c(\sigma)$ equals $n$ minus the number of cycles
of $\sigma$.  Its decomposition vector
$\mathbf{X}(\sigma)=(X_1(\sigma),\ldots,X_{n-1}(\sigma))$ is
\begin{equation}
X_j(\sigma) =
 \begin{cases}
   0 & \text{if $j$ is the largest item in its cycle in $\sigma$,} \\
   1 & \text{otherwise,}
  \end{cases}
 \label{eq:cayley_decomp}
\end{equation}
computable in $O(n)$.  For $\sigma = 2\,1\,3\,6\,4\,5$, whose cycle notation is
$(2\,1)(3)(4\,5\,6)$, one gets $\mathbf{X}(\sigma)=1\,0\,0\,1\,1$ and
$d_c(\sigma)=3$.  The functions \texttt{\_x\_vector\_cycles} and
\texttt{\_generate\_perm\_from\_x} implement the two directions of this
conversion, the latter being the core of the Cayley sampler.

\paragraph{Ulam distance.}
$d_u(\sigma,\pi)$ is $n$ minus the length of the longest common subsequence of
$\sigma$ and $\pi$; when the reference is the identity, $d_u(\sigma)$ is $n$
minus the length of the longest increasing subsequence (LIS).  The classical
illustration \citep{Diaconis:1988} is a shelf of books ordered as $\sigma$ that
must be reordered as $\pi$ using the minimum number of \emph{delete-and-insert}
movements; that minimum is exactly $d_u(\sigma,\pi)$.  For
$\sigma=2\,1\,3\,6\,4\,5\,7$ the LIS has length $5$, hence $d_u(\sigma)=2$.  The
implementation (\texttt{ulam\_distance}) computes the LIS in $O(n \log n)$ with
the patience-sorting algorithm.  \emph{No distance decomposition vector is known
for the Ulam distance}, which is why the generalized Mallows model cannot be
extended to it \citep{Irurozki_et_al:2014}.

\paragraph{Hamming distance.}
$d_H(\sigma,\pi) = |\{i : \sigma(i) \neq \pi(i)\}|$ counts the positions at
which the two permutations disagree.  It is the natural distance when the
absolute position of each item is what matters, as in the QAP, and it underlies
the kernels of Mallows models of Section~\ref{sec:kmm}.  The number of
permutations at Hamming distance $k$ from a fixed permutation is
$S(n,k) = \binom{n}{k} D_k$, where $D_k$ is the number of derangements of $k$
elements, computed by \texttt{compute\_derangements} through the recurrence
$D_k = (k-1)(D_{k-1}+D_{k-2})$.  Note that $d_H$ can never equal one.

\paragraph{Choice of distance.}
There is no distance that is best in general.  As reported by
\citet{Ceberio_et_al:2015}, some distances behave well for some problems
(meaning that they perform well for most instances of the problem) and other
distances for other problems.  Although an exhaustive correspondence between
distances and problems is not possible, as a general rule an EDA under a
particular distance will perform well on a problem when that metric is adequate
to measure the discrepancies between permutations in that domain.  The
correspondence suggested by the experimental study of the \texttt{perm\_mateda}
toolbox associates the Kendall's-$\tau$ distance with the PFSP, the Cayley
distance with the QAP, and the Ulam distance with the LOP.

\subsection{The Mallows Model}

The Mallows model \citep{Mallows:1957} is a distance-based exponential
probability model over permutation spaces.  Given a distance over permutations,
it is defined by two parameters: the central permutation $\sigma_0$ and the
spread parameter $\theta$.  The probability of every permutation $\sigma$ is
\begin{equation}
P(\sigma) = \psi(\theta)^{-1} \exp\big(-\theta\, d(\sigma, \sigma_0)\big) ,
\label{eq:mallows}
\end{equation}
where $\psi(\theta)$ is the normalization constant.  When $\theta>0$, $\sigma_0$
is the mode of the distribution, and the probability of the remaining $n!-1$
permutations decreases exponentially with their distance to $\sigma_0$: the
closer a permutation is to $\sigma_0$, the higher its probability.  When
$\theta=0$ the model reduces to the uniform distribution over $\mathcal{S}_n$,
and as $\theta$ grows the distribution concentrates around $\sigma_0$.  The
Mallows model is thus the exponential-family analogue, on $\mathcal{S}_n$, of the
Gaussian distribution on $\mathbb{R}^n$, with $\sigma_0$ playing the role of the
mean and $\theta^{-1}$ that of the variance.

\subsection{The Generalized Mallows Model}

The generalized Mallows (GM) model \citep{Fligner_and_Verducci:1986} is an
extension of the Mallows model which is also exponential and unimodal.  Instead
of a single spread parameter $\theta$, the GM model uses an $(n-1)$-dimensional
spread parameter $\bm{\theta} = (\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_{n-1})$, where each
$\theta_j$ affects a particular position of the permutation.  This allows the
distribution to put more emphasis on the consensus of certain positions while
keeping more uncertainty in others.

Not every distance that can be used in the Mallows model can be used in the GM
model.  The GM model requires the distance to decompose as a sum of $n-1$ terms,
\begin{equation}
d(\sigma, \sigma_0) = \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} S_j(\sigma\sigma_0^{-1}) ,
\label{eq:decomp}
\end{equation}
where the vector
$\mathbf{S}(\sigma\sigma_0^{-1}) = (S_1(\sigma\sigma_0^{-1}),\ldots,S_{n-1}(\sigma\sigma_0^{-1}))$
is the distance decomposition vector.  The GM model is then
\begin{equation}
P(\sigma) = \psi(\bm{\theta})^{-1}
  \exp\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} -\theta_j\, S_j(\sigma \sigma_0^{-1})\right) .
\label{eq:gmallows}
\end{equation}
The decomposition vector is $\mathbf{V}(\cdot)$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:kendall_decomp} for the Kendall's-$\tau$ distance and
$\mathbf{X}(\cdot)$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:cayley_decomp} for the Cayley distance.  In
both cases the interpretation of $\theta_j$ is the same: the larger $\theta_j$,
the larger the probability that position $j$ agrees with the consensus.  Since no
decomposition vector is known for the Ulam distance, \texttt{perm\_pateda}
provides five distance-based EDAs rather than six:
MEDA$_K$, MEDA$_C$, MEDA$_U$, GMEDA$_K$ and GMEDA$_C$.

\subsection{Learning the Mallows and Generalized Mallows Models}
\label{sec:learning_mallows}

Learning the parameters of a Mallows or GM model from a sample of permutations is
either known or conjectured to be NP-hard for all the distances considered here.
Within an EDA, however, the model that most accurately represents the selected
solutions need not be learned at every generation: approximate models can even be
beneficial, since they help to explore other areas of the search space, and they
are computationally much cheaper.  The learning step is therefore split into two
stages \citep{Irurozki:2014}:

\begin{enumerate}
 \item Approximate the central permutation $\sigma_0$.
 \item Obtain the maximum-likelihood estimate (MLE) of the spread
   parameter(s) $\bm{\theta}$ for the given distance.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Stage 1: central permutation.}
Three estimators are implemented in \texttt{consensus.py} and selected through
the \texttt{consensus\_method} argument of every Mallows/GM learner
(\texttt{"borda"}, the default; \texttt{"setmedian"}; \texttt{"best"}):

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Borda} \citep{Borda:1781} (\texttt{find\_consensus\_borda}):
  selects as central permutation the result of sorting the items according to
  their average position across all the input permutations.  When the
  permutations come from a Mallows model under the Kendall's-$\tau$ distance,
  the Borda permutation is an asymptotically optimal estimator of the central
  permutation \citep{Fligner_and_Verducci:1988}.  Although it can be used with
  any distance, it is recommended exclusively for the Kendall's-$\tau$
  distance.
\item \textbf{SetMedianPermutation} (\texttt{find\_consensus\_median}): selects
  the individual of the sample that minimizes the sum of distances to the rest,
  under the distance of the model.  It is $O(M^2)$ in the number $M$ of selected
  individuals and is the recommended default for the Cayley and Ulam distances.
\item \textbf{BestPermutation} (\texttt{find\_consensus\_best}): chooses the
  permutation with the best objective value.  This estimator requires the
  fitness values, which the learners receive as \texttt{selected\_fitness}.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The dispatcher \texttt{get\_consensus(method, population, fitness,
distance\_func)} implements the selection logic and raises an informative error
when the information required by an estimator (fitness for \texttt{"best"}, a
distance for \texttt{"setmedian"}) has not been supplied.

\paragraph{Stage 2: spread parameters.}
Once $\sigma_0$ has been obtained, the spread parameter(s) are estimated by
equating the derivative of the log-likelihood to zero.  The resulting expressions
differ for each distance, and in most cases a numerical method is used to
approximate the solution; exact expressions can be found in
\citet{Irurozki:2014,Irurozki_et_al:2016,Mandhani_and_Meila:2009}.
\texttt{perm\_pateda} uses:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{LearnMallowsKendall}: the observed mean inversion vector
    $\overline{\mathbf{V}}$ is matched against its expectation under the model,
    and a bounded scalar minimization (\texttt{scipy.optimize.fminbound}) over
    $\theta \in (0, \texttt{upper\_theta}]$ returns the MLE.  The
    normalization constants $\psi_j(\theta)$ are computed in closed form.
  \item \texttt{LearnGeneralizedMallowsKendall}: one $\theta_j$ per position is
    obtained by solving the corresponding univariate equation, giving the
    $(n-1)$-dimensional $\bm{\theta}$ together with the per-position probability
    matrix of the $V_j$ values used by the sampler.
  \item \texttt{LearnMallowsCayley} and
    \texttt{LearnGeneralizedMallowsCayley}: the mean of the cycle-based
    decomposition $\overline{\mathbf{X}}$ is used, and Newton's method
    (\texttt{scipy.optimize.newton}, with the analytic derivative) solves the
    likelihood equation; the resulting $\psi$ constants yield the vector of
    probabilities $P(X_j = 1)$ consumed by the sampler.
  \item \texttt{LearnMallowsUlam}: since the Ulam distance does not factorize,
    the learner matches the \emph{expected} distance under the model with the
    mean observed distance to $\sigma_0$.  This requires the number $N_d$ of
    permutations at each Ulam distance $d$, which is enumerated exactly for
    $n \leq 8$ and approximated by a Gaussian profile centred at
    $n - 2\sqrt{n}$ for larger $n$.
\end{itemize}

All learners return a plain Python dictionary, which is the model container used
throughout \texttt{perm\_pateda}; for instance
\texttt{\{"consensus": $\sigma_0$, "theta": $\theta$, "model\_type":
"mallows\_kendall", \ldots\}}.  This makes models trivially serializable and
inspectable (Section~\ref{sec:knowledge}).

\subsection{Sampling the Mallows and Generalized Mallows Models}

Sampling means generating permutations from the model obtained in the learning
stage, and the algorithm depends on the distance.

Mallows and GM models under the Kendall's-$\tau$ and Cayley distances
\emph{factorize}, which leads to efficient sampling algorithms.  Given the
per-position distributions obtained in the learning step, the sampler first draws
a decomposition vector ($\mathbf{V}$ for Kendall's-$\tau$, $\mathbf{X}$ for
Cayley) coordinate by coordinate, and then converts that vector into a
permutation, which is finally composed with $\sigma_0$.  This is the
\emph{multistage sampler} of \citet{Irurozki:2014}, implemented by
\texttt{SampleMallowsKendall}, \texttt{SampleMallowsCayley},
\texttt{SampleGeneralizedMallowsKendall} and
\texttt{SampleGeneralizedMallowsCayley}.  Its complexity is dominated by the
vector-to-permutation conversion, $O(n^2)$ for Kendall's-$\tau$ and $O(n)$ per
step for Cayley.

For the Ulam distance, sampling a permutation at a prescribed distance is
equivalent to sampling random standard Young tableaux, which is the
\emph{distances sampler} of \citet{Irurozki:2014}.  \texttt{perm\_pateda} takes a
simpler route: \texttt{SampleMallowsUlam} draws from
Eq.~\eqref{eq:mallows} with a Metropolis--Hastings Markov chain whose proposal is
a random transposition, accepting a move with probability
$\min\{1, \exp(-\theta\,\Delta d_u)\}$.  The chain is run for
\texttt{burn\_in} steps (default 1000 in \texttt{MallowsUlamEDA}) and one sample
is collected every \texttt{step\_size} steps (default 100).  When $\theta > 10$
the distribution is numerically degenerate and the sampler returns
\texttt{sample\_size} copies of $\sigma_0$, avoiding a needless chain.

\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np
from perm_pateda.learning.mallows import LearnMallowsCayley
from perm_pateda.sampling.mallows import SampleMallowsCayley

n, m = 10, 50
rng  = np.random.default_rng(0)
pop  = np.vstack([rng.permutation(n) for _ in range(m)])

learner = LearnMallowsCayley()
model = learner(generation=0, n_vars=n, cardinality=np.arange(n),
                selected_pop=pop, selected_fitness=np.zeros(m),
                consensus_method="setmedian")
print(model["model_type"], model["theta"])

sampler = SampleMallowsCayley()
new_pop = sampler(n_vars=n, model=model, cardinality=np.arange(n),
                  population=pop, fitness=np.zeros(m),
                  sample_size=100, rng=rng)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Kernels of Mallows Models under the Hamming Distance}
\label{sec:kmm}

Instead of estimating a single Mallows model centred on a consensus permutation,
a \emph{kernel} model \citep{Arza_et_al:2020} places one Mallows kernel on
\emph{each} selected solution and samples from the resulting mixture,
\begin{equation}
P(\sigma) = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}
   \psi(\theta)^{-1}\exp\big(-\theta\, d_H(\sigma, \sigma_i)\big) ,
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_M$ are the selected permutations.  This preserves
the diversity of the selected set, which a single unimodal model destroys, while
still concentrating the probability mass around good solutions.

\texttt{LearnHammingKMM} stores the selected population as the set of centres and
computes $\theta$ from a \emph{scheduled} expected distance rather than by
maximum likelihood.  The expected Hamming distance decreases exponentially
along the run, from $E_0$ (default $n/2$, parameter
\texttt{expected\_dist\_start}) to $E_{\max}$ (default $0.25$, parameter
\texttt{expected\_dist\_end}):
\begin{equation}
E(t) = E_{\max} + \delta(p)\,(E_0 - E_{\max}), \qquad
\delta(p) = \frac{e^{-\gamma p} - 1}{e^{-\gamma} - 1}, \qquad
p = \frac{t}{t_{\max}} ,
\end{equation}
with $\gamma$ (default $5.14$) controlling the decay rate.  The value of
$\theta$ matching a target expected distance is obtained by bisection on
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[d_H] = \frac{\sum_{k} k\, S(n,k)\, e^{-\theta k}}
                       {\sum_{k} S(n,k)\, e^{-\theta k}}, \qquad
S(n,k) = \binom{n}{k} D_k ,
\end{equation}
computed in log-space for numerical stability and excluding the impossible value
$k=1$.  \texttt{SampleHammingKMM} then implements the exact three-step sampler:
choose a centre $\sigma_0$ uniformly at random among the stored centres, draw a
distance $k$ with probability proportional to $S(n,k)e^{-\theta k}$, and build a
permutation at Hamming distance exactly $k$ from $\sigma_0$ by choosing $k$
positions and deranging them.  This schedule gives the algorithm an explicit
exploration-to-exploitation transition without any adaptive parameter.

\subsection{Histogram Models: EHM and NHM}

Histogram models summarize the selected population with a matrix of first- or
second-order statistics.  They were introduced for permutation problems by
\citet{Tsutsui:2002} and compared in \citet{Tsutsui_et_al:2006}.

\paragraph{Node histogram model (NHM).}
The NHM records how often each item appears at each position,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{NHM}[i,j] \;\propto\; \big|\{\sigma \in D^{Se} : \sigma(i) = j\}\big|
   + \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon = \beta_{\text{ratio}} \cdot M ,
\end{equation}
each row being normalized to a probability distribution.  The bias
$\varepsilon$, controlled by \texttt{beta\_ratio}, plays the role of a mutation
operator: it prevents zero probabilities and keeps every item reachable at every
position.  \texttt{SampleNHM} constructs a permutation position by position,
drawing the item at position $i$ from the (renormalized) row $i$ restricted to
the items not yet used, falling back to the uniform distribution over the
remaining items when the restricted row has zero mass.

\paragraph{Edge histogram model (EHM).}
The EHM records how often item $b$ immediately follows item $a$,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EHM}[a,b] \;=\;
   \big|\{\sigma \in D^{Se} : \exists\, i,\; \sigma(i)=a,\ \sigma(i+1)=b\}\big|
   \;+\; \varepsilon ,
\end{equation}
with $\varepsilon = \beta_{\text{ratio}}\,\frac{M}{n-1}$ in the asymmetric case
and $\varepsilon = \beta_{\text{ratio}}\,\frac{2M}{n-1}$ in the symmetric case
(\texttt{symmetric=True}, the default), where the matrix is replaced by
$\mathrm{EHM}+\mathrm{EHM}^\top$.  The symmetric variant is the appropriate one
for undirected problems such as the symmetric TSP, in which a tour and its
reverse are equivalent, while the asymmetric variant should be used when the
direction of a transition matters.  \texttt{SampleEHM} starts from a randomly
chosen item and extends the permutation greedily-at-random, at each step drawing
the next item from the row of the current item restricted to the unused items.
The EHM is a natural model for problems whose objective is defined on
\emph{adjacency} (TSP, PFSP), whereas the NHM suits problems defined on
\emph{absolute positions} (QAP, LOP).

\subsection{The Plackett--Luce Model}

The Plackett--Luce (PL) model \citep{Luce:1959,Plackett:1975} is a multistage
model in which the permutation is generated by sampling without replacement
proportionally to a vector of item weights $\mathbf{w} = (w_1,\ldots,w_n)$,
$w_i > 0$:
\begin{equation}
P(\sigma) = \prod_{i=1}^{n}
  \frac{w_{\sigma(i)}}{\sum_{j=i}^{n} w_{\sigma(j)}} .
\label{eq:pl}
\end{equation}
The parameter $w_i$ measures the propensity of item $i$ to be ranked early.
Unlike the Mallows model, the PL model is not defined through a distance, and it
can represent distributions that are not unimodal in the metric sense; it was
introduced in the EDA context by \citet{Ceberio_et_al:2013}.

\texttt{LearnPlackettLuce} computes the MLE of $\mathbf{w}$ with the
minorization--maximization (MM) algorithm of \citet{Hunter:2004}.  Writing $W_i$
for the number of ``wins'' of item $i$ (the number of stages in which $i$ is
chosen, i.e.\ the number of times $i$ appears in a position other than the last),
the MM update is
\begin{equation}
w_i \;\leftarrow\;
  W_i \Big/ \sum_{k=1}^{M} \sum_{p=1}^{\mathrm{pos}_k(i)}
  \Big(\sum_{q \geq p} w_{\sigma_k(q)}\Big)^{-1} ,
\end{equation}
followed by normalization.  The implementation computes the inner double sum in
$O(n)$ per ranking using suffix sums and cumulative sums, so one MM iteration
costs $O(Mn)$; iterations stop after \texttt{max\_iter} (default 100) or when the
$L_1$ change of $\mathbf{w}$ falls below \texttt{tol} (default $10^{-6}$).

\texttt{SamplePlackettLuce} exploits the \emph{Gumbel-max} trick: adding i.i.d.\
Gumbel noise to the log-weights and sorting the perturbed values produces exactly
a draw from Eq.~\eqref{eq:pl}.  Sampling a whole population is thus a single
vectorized \texttt{argsort} of an $(N,n)$ array, i.e.\ $O(Nn\log n)$, instead of
$N$ sequential loops of $n$ normalizations.

\subsection{Mixtures of Plackett--Luce Models}

A single PL model has one mode; when the selected population contains several
distinct groups of good solutions --- a common situation in multimodal
permutation problems --- a \emph{mixture} of $K$ PL components,
\begin{equation}
P(\sigma) = \sum_{c=1}^{K} \beta_c \, P(\sigma \mid \mathbf{w}^{(c)}), \qquad
\sum_c \beta_c = 1 ,
\end{equation}
is a far more faithful model.  \texttt{LearnPlackettLuceMixture} implements the
spectral-EM algorithm (EM-LSR) of \citet{Nguyen_and_Zhang:2023}, in two phases:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Spectral initialization.}  Every ranking is embedded as a binary
    pairwise-comparison vector of length $n(n-1)/2$, whose entry $(i,j)$ is $1$
    if $i$ precedes $j$.  A truncated SVD of the resulting $(M, n(n-1)/2)$ matrix
    is computed, the number of retained dimensions $\hat{r}$ is chosen adaptively
    from the gaps between singular values, and $k$-means clusters the projected
    rankings into $K$ groups.  A least-squares estimator then converts the
    empirical pairwise-comparison probabilities of each cluster into an initial
    parameter vector.
  \item \textbf{EM refinement with weighted LSR.}  The E-step computes the
    posterior responsibility of each component for each ranking, using
    log-sum-exp for stability; the M-step re-estimates each component with
    \emph{weighted Luce spectral ranking} \citep{Maystre_and_Grossglauser:2015},
    which is an exact MLE step rather than a surrogate, obtained as the
    stationary distribution of a Markov chain built from the weighted pairwise
    statistics.
\end{enumerate}

The defaults used inside an EDA are deliberately loose
(\texttt{max\_em\_iter=10}, \texttt{tol\_em=$10^{-4}$},
\texttt{max\_iter\_lsr=20}), since a rough model estimated cheaply at every
generation is preferable to an exact one estimated slowly.
\texttt{SamplePlackettLuceMixture} draws the component index from $\bm{\beta}$
and then applies the Gumbel-max trick with the corresponding weight vector,
again fully vectorized.  The number of components is exposed as
\texttt{n\_components} in \texttt{PlackettLuceMixtureEDA}.

\subsection{Doubly Stochastic Matrix Models}

A doubly stochastic matrix (DSM) $D \in [0,1]^{n\times n}$ --- all rows and all
columns summing to one --- is, by the Birkhoff--von Neumann theorem, a convex
combination of permutation matrices, and is therefore a natural parametric family
of distributions over $\mathcal{S}_n$ \citep{Santucci_and_Ceberio:2023}.

\texttt{LearnDSM} implements the smoothed learning scheme: the selected
permutations are converted to permutation matrices $P_1,\ldots,P_M$, averaged,
and mixed with the uniform DSM $U$ (all entries $1/n$),
\begin{equation}
D = \frac{1-\alpha}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M} P_i + \alpha\, U ,
\label{eq:dsm}
\end{equation}
where the smoothing factor $\alpha \in (0,1]$ prevents zero entries --- which
would make some assignments unreachable --- and defaults to $1/n^2$.
Equation~\eqref{eq:dsm} is the exact analogue, in permutation space, of the
Laplace-smoothed marginals used by the discrete EDAs of \texttt{pateda}.

Two samplers are provided:

\begin{description}
  \item[DSM-PS (probabilistic sampling)] builds one permutation iteratively: a
    still-unassigned row of $D$ is chosen uniformly at random, the column is
    drawn from the corresponding row probabilities renormalized over the
    unassigned columns, and the matched row and column are removed.  The
    probability of sampling $\sigma$ is
    $\Pr(\sigma \mid D) = \prod_i d_{i,\sigma(i)} / \mathrm{Perm}(D)$, where
    $\mathrm{Perm}(D)$ is the permanent of $D$.  Cost: $O(n^2)$ per permutation.
  \item[DSM-AS (algebraic sampling)] draws a uniform random vector $v$, computes
    $Dv$, and reads the permutation off the ranking relationship between $v$ and
    $Dv$.  It is also $O(n^2)$ per permutation, but the cost is a matrix--vector
    product, so it is fully vectorizable over the whole population and, in
    practice, considerably faster thanks to optimized BLAS routines.
\end{description}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Models over Bijective Codings of $\mathcal{S}_n$}{Models over Bijective Codings of Sn}}
\label{sec:representations}

The modules \texttt{representations/} exploit a different idea: rather than
defining a distribution on $\mathcal{S}_n$, map $\mathcal{S}_n$ bijectively onto
a product space of bounded integers, in which every vector is a valid code, and
then use a \emph{standard discrete} EDA model.  The abstract interface
\texttt{PermutationRepresentation} requires three methods:
\texttt{encode(perm)}, \texttt{decode(code)} and \texttt{get\_domain(n)}
(the inclusive maximum value allowed at each position).  All three
implementations are vectorized over a population.

\begin{description}
  \item[\texttt{LehmerRepresentation}] The Lehmer code (or factoradic
    representation) \citep{Lehmer:1960,RegnierCoudert_and_McCall:2014}.  In the
    right (default) variant, $c_i = |\{j > i : \sigma(j) < \sigma(i)\}|$, so
    $c_i \in \{0,\ldots,n-1-i\}$ and $c_{n-1}=0$; the left variant counts
    inversions to the left, giving $c_i \in \{0,\ldots,i\}$.  The right Lehmer
    code is precisely the inversion vector $\mathbf{V}$ of
    Eq.~\eqref{eq:kendall_decomp}, so a univariate model over Lehmer codes is
    closely related to a generalized Mallows model under the Kendall's-$\tau$
    distance centred on the identity.
  \item[\texttt{FisherYatesRepresentation}] The sequence of draws performed by
    the Fisher--Yates shuffle \citep{Fisher_and_Yates:1938,Durstenfeld:1964}: at
    step $i$, $c_i \in \{0,\ldots,n-1-i\}$ is the offset of the element swapped
    into position $i$.  Decoding replays the shuffle.
  \item[\texttt{InsertionVectorRepresentation}] The insertion vector, obtained as
    the left Lehmer code of the \emph{inverse} permutation, with
    $c_i \in \{0,\ldots,i\}$: $c_i$ is the position at which item $i$ is inserted
    into the partial permutation of items $0,\ldots,i-1$.  It is the coding
    underlying insertion-based neighbourhoods, which are the natural move
    operator for the LOP and the PFSP.
\end{description}

Three families of models are learned over these codings, all of them handling the
fact that the domain size varies with the position:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{LearnUMDA} / \texttt{SampleLehmerUMDA},
    \texttt{SampleFisherYatesUMDA}, \texttt{SampleInsertionVectorUMDA}:
    independent per-position marginals with Laplace smoothing
    (\texttt{laplace\_alpha}, default $0.01$ in the wrappers).  This is the
    UMDA \citep{Muhlenbein_and_Paas:1996} transported to permutation space.
  \item \texttt{LearnTree} / \texttt{SampleLehmerTree},
    \texttt{SampleFisherYatesTree}: a Chow--Liu tree \citep{Chow_and_Liu:1968}
    built from the pairwise mutual information between code positions, oriented
    from a user-selected \texttt{root}, with one conditional probability table
    per edge.  This is the Tree-EDA of \texttt{pateda} applied to a bijective
    coding, and it can capture dependencies between positions that the
    univariate model misses.
  \item \texttt{LearnInsertionVectorChain} /
    \texttt{SampleInsertionVectorChain}: a first-order Markov chain over the
    insertion vector, $P(c) = P(c_0)\prod_{i\geq 1} P(c_i \mid c_{i-1})$, with
    smoothed conditional tables of shape $(i, i+1)$.
\end{itemize}

Because \emph{every} code decodes to a permutation, no repair operator is ever
needed, and the learned model can be inspected with the same tools as a discrete
\texttt{pateda} model.

\subsection{Random-Key EDAs}

Random keys \citep{Bean:1994} encode a permutation as a real vector
$x \in [0,1]^n$; the permutation is recovered as the ordering induced by sorting
$x$.  Since every real vector decodes to a valid permutation, any
\emph{continuous} EDA can be used to search $\mathcal{S}_n$.  The module
\texttt{random\_keys.py} provides the conversions:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{9.2cm}}
\toprule
Function & Description \\
\midrule
\texttt{random\_keys\_to\_permutation(x)} & Stable \texttt{argsort} of the keys;
  works on a single vector or a whole population \\
\texttt{permutation\_to\_random\_keys(p)} & Assigns evenly spaced keys in
  $[0,1]$ consistent with the given order, with optional jitter \\
\texttt{random\_keys\_to\_ranks(x)} & 1-based ranks of the keys \\
\texttt{rescale\_random\_keys(x)} & Rank-based rescaling
  $x_i \leftarrow (\mathrm{rank}_i - 1)/(n-1)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The three algorithm classes \texttt{RKGaussianUMDAEDA},
\texttt{RKGaussianFullEDA} and \texttt{RKCopulaVinesEDA} implement the RK-EDA of
\citet{Ayodele_et_al:2016} with the continuous learners and samplers of
\texttt{pateda} (univariate Gaussian, full multivariate Gaussian, and vine
copulas respectively).  The two mechanisms that make RK-EDA effective are both
exposed as constructor flags:

\begin{description}
  \item[Diminishing variance / rescaling (\texttt{diminishing=True})] The
    selected keys are rank-rescaled to evenly spaced values in $[0,1]$ before the
    model is learned, and the sampled keys are rescaled again.  This removes the
    arbitrary scale of the keys and keeps the mapping to permutations
    well conditioned.
  \item[Cooling (\texttt{cooling=True})] The standard deviation used for sampling
    is not the one estimated from the data but a scheduled value
    \begin{equation}
    \sigma_g = \max\left(\sigma_0 \cdot \max\left(1 - \frac{g}{G},\,0\right),\,
                          \sigma_{\min}\right), \qquad
    \sigma_0 = \frac{1}{\pi \log_{10} n} ,
    \end{equation}
    which decreases linearly with the generation $g$.  The correlation structure
    learned by the model is preserved: for a full-covariance model the covariance
    matrix is converted to a correlation matrix and rescaled to the target
    variance.  For the vine-copula variant, which has no explicit variance
    parameter, Gaussian noise of standard deviation $\sigma_g$ is added to the
    sampled keys instead.
\end{description}

\subsection{Fourier-Based Models (experimental)}

The modules \texttt{multiobjective/meda\_d\_f.py},
\texttt{multiobjective/fourier\_moead.py} and
\texttt{multiobjective/moead.py} implement algorithms based on the Fourier
decomposition of permutation functions
\citep{Chicano_et_al:2023,Kondor_et_al:2007}, in which a pseudo-Boolean-like
spectral expansion of the objective over $\mathcal{S}_n$ is truncated at a small
order and used either as a surrogate for pre-screening candidates
(\texttt{FourierMOEAD}) or as a probability model to be combined across
objectives (\texttt{MEDA\_D\_F}).  These three modules import a
\texttt{perm\_pateda.fourier} package (and the auxiliary modules
\texttt{perm\_pateda.distributions} and \texttt{perm\_pateda.utils.permutations})
that are \emph{not part of the current distribution}: they come from the separate
\texttt{permutation\_fourier} project.  Consequently they are not re-exported by
\texttt{perm\_pateda.multiobjective.\_\_init\_\_} and are not importable out of
the box.  They are documented here for completeness and should be regarded as
experimental until that dependency is vendored or declared.

