%% ============================================================
\section{EDA Components}
\label{sec:components}
%% ============================================================

Apart from learning and sampling, all the components of an EDA are
representation agnostic and are inherited unchanged from \texttt{pateda}.  This
section states which ones apply to permutations and documents the single
component that \texttt{perm\_pateda} contributes.

\subsection{Seeding Methods}

\texttt{PermutationInit} (\texttt{seeding/permutation\_init.py}) is the
permutation counterpart of \texttt{RandomInit}: it generates \texttt{pop\_size}
permutations of $\mathtt{range(n\_vars)}$ drawn uniformly at random, i.e.\ a
sample from the uniform distribution over $\mathcal{S}_n$, which is the Mallows
model with $\theta = 0$.  It implements the \texttt{pateda}
\texttt{SeedingMethod} interface, so it can be used both from the wrappers and
from \texttt{EDAComponents}.  Biased initialization --- seeding the population
with solutions produced by a construction heuristic --- is discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:apriori}.

\subsection{Selection Methods}

All \texttt{pateda} selection operators apply directly, since selection only
depends on the fitness values: truncation, proportional, tournament, Boltzmann,
and the multi-objective schemes.  The plug-and-play wrappers hard-code
\emph{truncation selection} with ratio \texttt{selection\_ratio}, which is the
standard choice in the permutation EDA literature (the experimental study of
\citet{Irurozki_et_al:2018} selects the best $10\%$ of a population of size
$10n$).  Use the component path to employ any other operator.

\subsection{Replacement Methods}

Likewise, all \texttt{pateda} replacement operators are usable.  The wrappers
implement \emph{elitism} directly: the best permutation found so far is
reinserted in place of the worst individual of the new population whenever the
new population does not improve on it.  Population aggregation --- adding the
$N$ new solutions to the current population and keeping the best $N$, the
replacement used in \citet{Irurozki_et_al:2018} --- is available as
\texttt{pateda.replacement.ElitistReplacement} through the component path.

\subsection{Stopping Conditions}

Inherited from \texttt{pateda}: maximum number of generations, maximum number of
evaluations, target fitness, and stagnation-based criteria.  The wrappers use a
fixed number of generations (\texttt{n\_gen}).

\subsection{Local Optimization}

\texttt{pateda} local search operators that assume a discrete Cartesian
representation (bit-flip hill climbing, and the like) are \emph{not} applicable
to permutations.  Permutation local search must use permutation neighbourhoods
--- swap, insertion (\emph{jump}), or 2-opt --- and can be supplied as a custom
\texttt{LocalOptimizationMethod} through \texttt{EDAComponents}.  The
multi-objective algorithms of Section~\ref{sec:multiobjective} accept an external
perturbation operator directly through the \texttt{mutation\_fn} argument
(a callable \texttt{(perm, rng) -> perm}) and its application probability
\texttt{mutation\_rate}, and additionally implement a \emph{shaking} mechanism:
after \texttt{shake\_threshold} generations without improvement, a subproblem's
incumbent is perturbed with \texttt{shake\_strength} random insertion moves.

\subsection{Repairing Methods}

Repair operators, which are essential when a model may generate infeasible
solutions, are \emph{unnecessary} in \texttt{perm\_pateda}: every sampler returns
a valid permutation by construction, either because the model is defined on
$\mathcal{S}_n$ and sampled sequentially without replacement, or because the
representation is a bijective coding, or because the random-key decoding is a
sort.  This is the central design advantage of permutation-specific models over
the naive use of an integer-vector EDA.

\subsection{Mutation and Crossover}

\texttt{perm\_pateda} does not implement permutation crossover operators
(order crossover, PMX, cycle crossover) as first-class components; where a
crossover is needed --- for example in the candidate-generation step of the
Fourier-assisted multi-objective algorithms --- it is implemented locally in the
corresponding module.  Permutation mutation is available through the
\texttt{mutation\_fn} hook described above.

%% ============================================================
\section{Test Functions and Benchmark Problems}
\label{sec:functions}
%% ============================================================

The \texttt{functions/} package contains seven permutation problems.  Each
problem is a class whose \texttt{\_\_call\_\_} returns a value to be
\emph{maximized} (Section~\ref{sec:eda}), accompanied by random-instance
generators and, where applicable, a loader for the standard benchmark format.
Following the organization of \texttt{perm\_mateda}, each problem separates
instance reading from solution evaluation.

\subsection{Classical Permutation Problems}

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP)}]
    (\texttt{functions/tsp.py}).  An instance is an $n \times n$ matrix of
    distances between cities.  A permutation is a Hamiltonian cycle and its cost
    is
    $\sum_{i=1}^{n} D[\sigma(i), \sigma(i \bmod n + 1)]$,
    including the wrap-around edge; \texttt{\_\_call\_\_} returns its negation.
    The implementation validates that the matrix is symmetric.  Generators:
    \texttt{create\_random\_tsp(n\_cities, seed)} (random symmetric matrix) and
    \texttt{create\_tsp\_from\_coordinates(coords)} (Euclidean distances from a
    set of 2-D points).  Reader: \texttt{parse\_tsplib} \citep{Reinelt:1991}.
  \item[\textbf{Permutation Flowshop Scheduling Problem (PFSP)}]
    (\texttt{functions/pfsp.py}).  An instance is an $n \times m$ matrix of
    processing times, $P[i,j]$ being the time job $i$ spends on machine $j$; all
    jobs traverse the machines in the same order, and a permutation fixes the
    order of the jobs.  Two objectives are supported through the
    \texttt{objective} argument: \texttt{"makespan"}, the completion time of the
    last job on the last machine, and \texttt{"flowtime"}, the sum of the
    completion times of all jobs.  Both are computed from the standard
    completion-time recurrence
    $C_{i,j} = \max(C_{i-1,j}, C_{i,j-1}) + P[\sigma(i), j]$, and returned
    negated.  Generator: \texttt{create\_random\_pfsp}; reader:
    \texttt{parse\_taillard\_pfsp} \citep{Taillard:1993} together with
    \texttt{load\_taillard\_instance}.  The distance-based EDA of
    \citet{Ceberio_et_al:2014} was designed for this problem.
  \item[\textbf{Linear Ordering Problem (LOP)}]
    (\texttt{functions/lop.py}).  An instance is an $n \times n$ matrix $B$; the
    objective is to find the permutation maximizing
    $\sum_{i<j} B[\sigma(i), \sigma(j)]$, i.e.\ the sum of the entries above the
    diagonal of the permuted matrix \citep{Grotschel_et_al:1984,Marti_and_Reinelt:2011}.
    It has applications in ranking and aggregation, input--output economics,
    archaeological seriation and the feedback arc set problem.  Generators:
    \texttt{create\_random\_lop}, \texttt{create\_tournament\_lop} (a random
    tournament, where the optimum is a ranking), \texttt{create\_triangular\_lop}
    (an instance with a planted optimum), \texttt{create\_sparse\_lop} (with a
    prescribed density), plus \texttt{feedback\_arc\_set\_to\_lop} which converts
    a directed graph into the equivalent LOP.  Reader: \texttt{parse\_lolib}
    together with \texttt{load\_lolib\_instance}.
  \item[\textbf{Quadratic Assignment Problem (QAP)}]
    (\texttt{functions/qap.py}).  An instance consists of a flow matrix $F$
    between facilities and a distance matrix $D$ between locations
    \citep{Koopmans_and_Beckmann:1957,Cela:1998}; a permutation assigns
    facilities to locations and its cost is
    $\sum_{i,j} F[i,j]\, D[\sigma(i), \sigma(j)]$, returned negated by default
    (\texttt{minimize=True}).  Generators: \texttt{create\_random\_qap} (with an
    optional sparse flow matrix), \texttt{create\_uniform\_qap} and
    \texttt{create\_grid\_qap} (locations on a regular grid, a structured and
    notoriously hard family).  Reader: \texttt{parse\_qaplib}
    \citep{Burkard_et_al:1997} together with \texttt{load\_qaplib\_instance}.
\end{description}

\subsection{Graph Problems under the Permutation Picture}

Three classical graph problems are included in the formulation of
\citet{Min:2024}, in which a permutation $\sigma$ of the vertices together with a
cut point $k$ encodes a subset of vertices, and the constraint is expressed
through the trace $\mathrm{Tr}(P A P^\top C(k))$ of the permuted adjacency matrix
against a truncation matrix $C(k)$.  This reformulation turns three subset
selection problems into permutation problems, so that any permutation EDA can be
applied to them.

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Maximum Independent Set (MIS)}] (\texttt{functions/mis.py}).
    Maximize $k$ subject to $\mathrm{Tr}(P A P^\top C(k)) = 0$, with $C(k)$ the
    $k\times k$ upper-left block of ones.  A permutation is evaluated by scanning
    it and greedily accumulating the vertices that are not adjacent to any
    already selected vertex; the fitness is the size of the resulting independent
    set.  Auxiliary methods: \texttt{evaluate\_independent\_set} returns the set
    itself and \texttt{is\_valid\_independent\_set} verifies it.  Generators:
    \texttt{create\_random\_mis(n, edge\_probability, seed)} and
    \texttt{create\_mis\_from\_edges(n, edges)}.
  \item[\textbf{Maximum Cut (MaxCut)}] (\texttt{functions/maxcut.py}).  Maximize
    $\tfrac12\mathrm{Tr}(P A P^\top C(k))$ with
    $C(k)_{ij} = 1$ iff $(i \leq k < j)$ or $(j \leq k < i)$.  A permutation is
    evaluated by trying all $n-1$ cut points and returning the largest cut found,
    so that the encoded solution is the pair (permutation, best cut point).
    \texttt{evaluate\_cut} additionally returns the cut point and the two vertex
    subsets.  Generators: \texttt{create\_random\_max\_cut},
    \texttt{create\_max\_cut\_from\_edges}.
  \item[\textbf{Minimum Vertex Cover (MVC)}] (\texttt{functions/mvc.py}).
    Minimize $k$ subject to $\mathrm{Tr}(P A P^\top C(k)) = 0$ with
    $C(k)_{ij} = 1$ iff $i>k$ and $j>k$, i.e.\ no edge lies entirely outside the
    first $k$ vertices.  The fitness returned is $-k$, consistent with the
    maximization convention.  Generators: \texttt{create\_random\_mvc},
    \texttt{create\_mvc\_from\_edges}.
\end{description}

\subsection{Benchmark Instance Readers}

The module \texttt{utils/benchmark\_parsers.py} implements readers for the four
standard benchmark libraries used in the permutation EDA literature.  Each
returns plain \texttt{numpy} arrays that can be fed directly to the corresponding
problem class:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Function & Library & Returns \\
\midrule
\texttt{parse\_lolib(path)} & LOLIB (LOP) & $(n,n)$ weight matrix \\
\texttt{parse\_taillard\_pfsp(path)} & Taillard (PFSP) & $(n_{\text{jobs}}, n_{\text{machines}})$ times \\
\texttt{parse\_qaplib(path)} & QAPLIB (QAP) & (flow, distance) matrices \\
\texttt{parse\_tsplib(path)} & TSPLIB (TSP) & $(n,n)$ distance matrix \\
\bottomrule
