\section{Multi-Objective Optimization}
\label{sec:multiobjective}
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Multi-Objective Problem Definition}

A multi-objective permutation problem requires the simultaneous optimization of
$m$ objectives $f_1(\sigma), \ldots, f_m(\sigma)$ over $\mathcal{S}_n$.  Since
the objectives usually conflict, the goal is the \emph{Pareto front}: the set of
non-dominated permutations, for which no objective can be improved without
degrading another.  In \texttt{perm\_pateda} the objectives are supplied as a
list of callables, each mapping a 0-indexed permutation to a float, together with
a tuple of \texttt{minimize} flags (one per objective, all \texttt{True} by
default), which allows mixing minimization and maximization objectives in a
single problem.

\subsection{Pareto Dominance and Archive}

The module \texttt{multiobjective/pareto.py} provides
\texttt{dominates(a, b, minimize)}, \texttt{pareto\_front(objectives, minimize)}
--- returning the indices of the non-dominated rows --- and the class
\texttt{ParetoArchive}, an unbounded external archive.  \texttt{ParetoArchive.add}
inserts a solution only if it is not dominated by an existing member and is not a
numerical duplicate of one, and removes every member that the new solution
dominates.  \texttt{get\_front()} returns the pair (solutions, objectives).

\subsection{Scalarization}

The module \texttt{multiobjective/scalarization.py} implements the two
scalarization functions used by decomposition-based algorithms:
\begin{align}
g^{ws}(\sigma \mid \mathbf{w}) &= \sum_{i=1}^{m} w_i f_i(\sigma), \\
g^{te}(\sigma \mid \mathbf{w}, \mathbf{z}^\ast) &=
  \max_{1 \leq i \leq m} w_i\, |f_i(\sigma) - z_i^\ast| ,
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{z}^\ast$ is the ideal (reference) point.  The Tchebycheff
formulation guarantees that every Pareto-optimal solution can be recovered by
some weight vector, which the weighted sum does not; it is the default in all the
MEDA/D algorithms.  Internally these algorithms use \emph{normalized} variants,
in which the objectives are rescaled by the current $(\mathbf{z}^\ast,
\mathbf{z}^{\text{nad}})$ box, so that objectives on very different scales
(as in bi-objective TSP/QAP combinations) contribute comparably.

\subsection{The MEDA/D Family}

\texttt{perm\_pateda} implements the multi-objective decomposition-based EDA
framework of \citet{Zangari_et_al:2017}, which combines the MOEA/D decomposition
scheme \citep{Zhang_and_Li:2007} with permutation probability models.  The
multi-objective problem is decomposed into $N$ scalar subproblems defined by
uniformly spread weight vectors; each subproblem $k$ keeps one incumbent
solution, and its neighbourhood $B(k)$ consists of the $T$ subproblems with the
closest weight vectors.  At each generation, and for each subproblem, a model is
estimated from the neighbourhood (or a kernel is centred on the incumbent), one
new permutation is sampled from it, evaluated, and used to replace up to
\texttt{nr} neighbours that it improves under their scalarized objective.  The
external \texttt{ParetoArchive} accumulates the non-dominated solutions found.

The framework is instantiated with nine models, all sharing the same interface:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Class} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Module} \\
\midrule
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_MK} & Kernels of Mallows models, Cayley distance & \texttt{meda\_d\_mk} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_KENDALL} & Mallows, Kendall's-$\tau$ & \texttt{meda\_d\_mk} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_ULAM} & Mallows, Ulam & \texttt{meda\_d\_mk} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_GMKENDALL} & Generalized Mallows, Kendall's-$\tau$ & \texttt{meda\_d\_mk} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_GMCAYLEY} & Generalized Mallows, Cayley & \texttt{meda\_d\_mk} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_PLACKETT\_LUCE} & Plackett--Luce & \texttt{meda\_d\_pl} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_MIXTURE\_PLACKETT\_LUCE} & Mixture of Plackett--Luce & \texttt{meda\_d\_pl} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_NHM} & Node histogram model & \texttt{meda\_d\_hm} \\
\texttt{MEDA\_D\_EHM} & Edge histogram model & \texttt{meda\_d\_hm} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All of them accept the constructor parameters \texttt{objectives}, \texttt{n},
\texttt{n\_subproblems} ($N$), \texttt{neighbourhood\_size} ($T$), \texttt{nr},
\texttt{scalarization}, \texttt{shake\_threshold}, \texttt{shake\_strength},
\texttt{minimize}, \texttt{seed}, and the optional external perturbation
operator \texttt{mutation\_fn} with its probability \texttt{mutation\_rate}
(disabled by default).  \texttt{MEDA\_D\_MK} additionally takes the fixed kernel
spread \texttt{theta}; the histogram variants take \texttt{selection\_ratio} and
\texttt{beta\_ratio}; the mixture variant takes the number of components.  The
method
\texttt{run(n\_generations, verbose, initial\_population, generation\_callback)}
returns a dictionary with the keys \texttt{pareto\_solutions},
\texttt{pareto\_objectives}, \texttt{final\_population} and
\texttt{final\_objectives}.  The \texttt{generation\_callback} hook makes it easy
to track quality indicators along the run.

\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np
from perm_pateda.multiobjective import MEDA_D_GMCAYLEY
from perm_pateda.functions import create_random_qap, create_random_lop

n = 20
qap = create_random_qap(n, seed=1)
lop = create_random_lop(n, seed=2)

# both objectives expressed as minimization
objectives = [lambda p: -qap(p), lambda p: -lop(p)]

alg = MEDA_D_GMCAYLEY(
    objectives=objectives, n=n,
    n_subproblems=50, neighbourhood_size=10, nr=2,
    scalarization="tchebycheff",
    minimize=(True, True), seed=0,
)
result = alg.run(n_generations=200, verbose=True)
print("Pareto front size:", len(result["pareto_solutions"]))
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Relation to the \texttt{pateda} Multi-Objective Module}

\texttt{pateda} addresses multi-objective problems mainly by modifying the
selection scheme (non-dominated sorting, crowding distance, indicator-based
selection) and provides quality indicators, an external archive and a MOEA/D
implementation; see the corresponding section of the \texttt{pateda} user guide.
Those components can be combined with the permutation learners and samplers
through \texttt{EDAComponents} whenever a Pareto-selection-based rather than a
decomposition-based algorithm is desired.  The MEDA/D classes described above are
self-contained implementations that do not go through the \texttt{pateda}
executor, because the model is re-estimated per subproblem rather than once per
generation.

%% ============================================================
\section{Model Inspection and Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:knowledge}
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Inspecting the Learned Models}

Because every learner returns a plain dictionary, the model is directly
inspectable and can be logged at every generation.  The most informative
quantities are:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{8.6cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Key} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
\texttt{consensus} & Central permutation $\sigma_0$ of a Mallows/GM model; its
  evolution shows which orderings the search converges to \\
\texttt{theta} & Spread parameter; the transition from small to large $\theta$
  marks the exploration-to-exploitation switch of the run \\
\texttt{thetas} & The $(n-1)$ per-position spreads of a GM model; positions with
  large $\theta_j$ have already converged \\
\texttt{ehm\_matrix}, \texttt{nhm\_matrix} & Edge/node frequency matrices; their
  entropy is a direct diversity measure \\
\texttt{weights} & Plackett--Luce item weights, i.e.\ an estimated ranking \\
\texttt{dsm} & Doubly stochastic matrix; its distance to a permutation matrix
  measures convergence \\
\texttt{marginals}, \texttt{conditionals} & Tables of the models over bijective
  codings, in the same format used by \texttt{pateda} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Tracking $\theta$ across generations reproduces the two-phase behaviour reported
by \citet{Irurozki_et_al:2018}: in the first generations $\theta$ takes very low
values, denoting a large spread among the solutions in the population
(exploration), and later it increases rapidly, indicating convergence
(exploitation).  At that point the probability of sampling a solution far from
the consensus becomes extremely low, so practitioners may wish to cap the maximum
$\theta$ (parameter \texttt{upper\_theta} of the learners) to delay stagnation.

Since the models over bijective codings (Section~\ref{sec:representations}) are
ordinary discrete probabilistic graphical models, the whole
\texttt{knowledge\_extraction} module of \texttt{pateda} --- dependency measures,
network measures, structure visualization --- applies to them without
modification.

\subsection{Statistical Comparison Utilities}

The module \texttt{utils/stats\_utils.py} implements the comparison protocol used
in the permutation EDA literature \citep{Ceberio_et_al:2015,Demsar:2006}.  Its
input is a \texttt{pandas} \texttt{DataFrame} whose rows are problem instances
and whose columns are algorithms:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{8.6cm}}
\toprule
Function & Description \\
\midrule
\texttt{summary\_table(df)} & Mean $\pm$ standard deviation, best value and
  average rank per algorithm \\
\texttt{friedman\_test(df)} & Friedman omnibus test across algorithms; returns
  the statistic, the $p$-value and the average ranks \\
\texttt{wilcoxon\_pairwise(df)} & Matrix of pairwise Wilcoxon signed-rank
  $p$-values \\
\texttt{critical\_difference\_plot(df, alpha, filepath)} & Critical-difference
  diagram (Nemenyi) saved to file \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

%% ============================================================
\section{Injecting A-Priori Knowledge}
\label{sec:apriori}
%% ============================================================

\texttt{pateda} offers several mechanisms to inject a-priori problem-structure
information into an EDA: fixed factorizations, interaction matrices constraining
the structures a learner may consider, biased initialization, and constrained
search.  Their general description is given in the \texttt{pateda} user guide.
In the permutation setting they specialize as follows.

\begin{description}
  \item[Biased initialization] Any set of permutations --- for instance produced
    by a construction heuristic such as NEH for the PFSP or nearest-neighbour for
    the TSP --- can be passed as the initial population.  The multi-objective
    algorithms accept it directly through the \texttt{initial\_population}
    argument of \texttt{run()}; for the single-objective wrappers, replace the
    seeding component through \texttt{EDAComponents}.
  \item[Structural priors] The tree models over bijective codings accept a
    \texttt{root} argument that fixes the orientation of the dependency tree; a
    problem-informed choice of root (e.g.\ the position known to be most
    constrained) acts as a mild structural prior.  Restricting the set of
    candidate edges through an interaction matrix, as \texttt{pateda} does for
    Tree-EDA\_r, transfers directly to \texttt{LearnTree}.
  \item[Model priors] The smoothing parameters play the role of a prior over the
    model: \texttt{beta\_ratio} in the histogram models, \texttt{alpha} in the
    DSM models, and \texttt{laplace\_smoothing} in the coding-based models.
    Larger values keep more of the uniform distribution in the mixture and thus
    slow down convergence.  In the Mallows kernels, the schedule
    $(E_0, E_{\max}, \gamma)$ is an explicit, problem-independent prior on the
    exploration/exploitation trade-off.
  \item[Choice of distance and model] The strongest form of a-priori knowledge in
    a permutation EDA is the choice of the distance or model family itself, since
    it determines which discrepancies between permutations the search considers
    small.  Section~\ref{sec:models} summarizes the recommended correspondences.
\end{description}

